:root {
    /* Palette and semantic states, taken from the board.

       Every colour below is the arena's own, read out of the materials in
       Assets/Materials rather than picked to look nice beside them:

         Map_DeckA      #A8B3B8   the deck the game is played on
         Map_DeckB      #9FAAB0   its alternate tile
         Map_GridLine   #6E7A80   the line between cells
         Map_Cover      #46525A   the walls
         Map_CoverPlate #E8E2D4   the warm bone plate on top of them
         Map_Void       #5E5346   the table the whole board sits on
         Map_WallCap    #ADC7D1   the pale cap catching the sky
         MoveOverlayCell #3085EE  "you can act here"

       That last one matters more than the rest. The board already has a colour
       that means a reachable cell, and the interface already needed a colour
       that means a chosen row. Making them the same one means a player learns
       the language once instead of twice.

       Four moves, and every value serves one:

       1. Paper, not plates. Panels are the deck, lightened, and translucent
          over a lit stage.
       2. Structure is drawn in line, not in fill — a control is bounded by a
          grid-line hairline, and it darkens when you touch it.
       3. One warm accent owns the objective and the next action. It is the only
          warm thing in the chrome, exactly as the bone plates and the table are
          the only warm things on an otherwise cool board.
       4. The chosen thing inverts, to the board's own overlay blue with white
          ink.

       Names describe roles, not hues, which is what keeps letting this be a
       change of values: --toy-select has held an indigo, a white plate, a
       borrowed blue, and now the board's own, and no call site has moved.

       Two things here are NOT free to move. The team pair mirrors
       TeamPalette.cs, which also paints unit bodies and tracers on the board;
       the health ramp mirrors TeamPalette's thresholds. Change either here and
       you must change it there in the same edit. */

    /* Ground and surfaces. The deck, walked up toward white so type can sit on
       it: --toy-surface is DeckA lightened, --toy-surface-hover is DeckA itself,
       and --toy-surface-pressed is DeckB. The interface is literally made of
       the floor.

       Elevation runs light-to-dark on paper: raised is nearer white, and a well
       is darker than the sheet it is cut into. Measured as WCAG contrast against
       --toy-surface the ladder steps 1.07 (raised), 1.33 (hover) and 1.09
       (quiet).

       The whole ladder was re-anchored when --toy-ground moved to #B4C1C5. The
       old ground was #CED6D9 and the surfaces above it ran up to #F1F4F5, which
       against the new, darker page read as pure white — the unselected cards on
       the Join screen glared. Shifting every step by the same delta keeps the
       ladder's relative structure and the design's "made of the floor" reading
       intact; raised is still the lightest thing on the page, just no longer
       white. The wells go darker with it, which is the direction they already
       ran. */
    --toy-ground: rgb(180, 193, 197);
    --toy-surface: rgb(202, 212, 215);
    --toy-surface-raised: rgb(215, 223, 225);
    --toy-surface-hover: rgb(142, 158, 164);
    --toy-surface-pressed: rgb(133, 149, 156);
    --toy-surface-quiet: rgb(191, 202, 205);
    /* Wells are recessed, so they run toward the grid line rather than away. */
    --toy-inset: rgb(165, 179, 183);
    /* Portrait and thumbnail backing: Map_CoverPlate exactly. The bone plate is
       the one warm surface on the board, and a cut-out character standing on it
       reads as standing on cover. */
    --toy-warm: rgb(232, 226, 212);
    /* An overlay lightens the game behind it toward the deck rather than dimming
       it toward black. */
    --toy-scrim: rgba(180, 193, 197, 0.84);
    --toy-panel: rgba(202, 212, 215, 0.9);
    /* For a plate that already sits on a scrim. Stacking a 0.90 panel on a 0.84
       scrim is opaque twice over and the dialog stops being a layer over the
       game and becomes a window in front of it. */
    --toy-panel-sheer: rgba(207, 217, 219, 0.8);
    --toy-transparent: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0);
    /* The masks are white with their shape in alpha, so each is tinted at the
       point of use and both themes share the files. The frame falls off to
       Map_Void — the interface ends where the table the board sits on begins. */
    --toy-scrim-tint: rgb(191, 203, 206);
    --toy-vignette-tint: rgb(111, 100, 85);

    /* Line work. Map_GridLine at low alpha. The board draws a cell boundary with
       exactly this colour, so a panel edge and a grid edge are the same mark at
       different scales. --toy-edge clears 2.2:1 on paper and the hover line
       4.6:1; --toy-divider stays quiet because it separates content rather than
       bounding a control. */
    --toy-edge: rgba(110, 122, 128, 0.42);
    --toy-edge-strong: rgba(70, 82, 90, 0.68);
    --toy-divider: rgba(110, 122, 128, 0.26);
    --toy-disabled-surface: rgb(212, 218, 220);
    --toy-disabled-border: rgba(110, 122, 128, 0.3);

    /* Text. Map_Cover, darkened until it carries at body size — the ink is the
       walls. De-emphasis walks back up toward the grid line rather than fading
       toward the paper.

       The muted step is bounded by contrast, not by taste. It labels real
       content — section headings, stat keys, unit descriptions, the map caption
       — so it has to clear 4.5:1 on every paper surface a label can land on,
       and the darkest of those is --toy-inset. At the grid line it managed 2.7
       there and 3.7 on the sheet, which is not a quiet label, it is an
       unreadable one. De-emphasis is carried by size, weight and tracking
       instead; the value only steps far enough to read as a second voice.
       Measured on --toy-surface: text 12.2, copy 7.3, muted 5.8. */
    --toy-text: rgb(31, 40, 46);
    --toy-text-copy: rgb(62, 76, 84);
    --toy-text-muted: rgb(78, 90, 97);
    /* Ink for the saturated fills. --toy-text-on-accent rides the orange;
       --toy-text-on-deep rides the overlay-blue select plate and every other
       saturated fill; --toy-text-on-danger and --toy-text-on-team ride the two
       fills that are too light for dark ink and too dark for the theme's own.

       The last two exist because --toy-text-on-deep flips with the theme, and a
       red button or a team badge does not: both stay saturated in the HUD, so a
       token that turns to near-black there puts 3.8:1 ink on a red plate. */
    --toy-text-on-accent: rgb(28, 18, 2);
    /* Off-white, not white. The select plate is a large filled area and pure
       white on it glares; this is as far back toward the HUD's off-white as the
       plate will carry, at 4.6:1.

       The other two stay pure white and cannot follow. Their fills are lighter
       and fixed — the team red mirrors TeamPalette and paints units on the
       board, so it cannot be darkened to make room — and white is already only
       4.5 on the enemy badge. Both are small marks rather than fields of
       colour, so they are not what a long match tires the eye with. */
    --toy-text-on-deep: rgb(232, 236, 238);
    --toy-text-on-danger: rgb(255, 255, 255);
    --toy-text-on-team: rgb(255, 255, 255);
    /* Secondary ink on the select plate, for the line under a chosen option's
       name. Held near white: the plate below is dark enough to carry it, and
       stepping it down toward the blue is what made it 2.9:1. */
    --toy-text-on-accent-quiet: rgb(225, 240, 252);
    --toy-text-border: rgba(110, 122, 128, 0.42);
    --toy-text-wash: rgba(70, 82, 90, 0.1);
    /* The primary button used to be the one place white sat on the orange
       rather than ink, propped up by --toy-shadow-ink. It measured 2.4:1, and a
       drop shadow does not make a ratio: it thickens the glyph's edge and
       leaves the stroke itself the same distance from the fill. This is the
       loudest, most-pressed control on every screen — Play, Create Match,
       Confirm, Lock In — so it now takes the same dark ink every other orange
       mark already used, at 7.6:1, and the shadow comes off with it. */
    --toy-text-on-primary: rgb(28, 18, 2);
    --toy-shadow-ink: rgba(10, 7, 2, 0.65);
    /* Display type over a lit 3D stage. The halo runs the same direction as the
       paper, so on this theme it is a light bloom holding navy capitals off a
       dark model rather than a dark one holding white capitals off a bright sky.
 
       Kept well under the dark theme's strength, and for a reason that only
       shows up on paper: a bloom the same colour as the page does not sit
       behind small text, it eats it. Anything at label size drops the halo
       entirely and relies on ink value instead. */
    --toy-shadow-stage: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.55);

    /* Primary: the objective and the next decision.
 
       Battle Plan's own Pogo Orange, kept from the previous theme on purpose.
       The borrowed idea here is structural — one warm hue in an otherwise cool
       interface, which is what lets a single orange pip read as "look here"
       from across the screen. Wearing the reference's literal brand orange
       would have made that borrowing look like a costume instead. */
    --toy-primary: rgb(241, 143, 1);
    --toy-primary-hover: rgb(255, 167, 40);
    --toy-primary-pressed: rgb(198, 116, 0);
    /* The accent as *text* on paper. The fill's own value only reaches 2.2:1
       against the lightened deck, so the copy variant has to go down rather than
       up — the opposite direction from the dark theme, where it was lifted.
       Far enough down to clear 4.5:1 on the whole surface ladder rather than
       only on the sheet: it also lands on --toy-surface-quiet, where the
       previous step measured 4.2. */
    --toy-primary-copy: rgb(135, 76, 0);
    /* Card state fills: the bone plate warmed a step further toward the accent,
       under an orange rule and an orange label that carry the actual state. */
    --toy-primary-tint: rgb(243, 231, 210);
    --toy-primary-wash: rgb(237, 233, 222);

    /* Select: the board's overlay blue, the colour a reachable cell is painted
       with. A chosen mode, unit, slot or tab inverts to it with white ink — the
       loudest state available, and unmistakably different from the orange that
       means "act on this". Selection is interaction only; team identity has its
       own pair further down.

       MoveOverlayCell is #3085EE at alpha 0.5, and the plate used to wear that
       hex at full strength. The cell never renders there — half-covering the
       deck lands it near #6C9CD3 — so matching the number was matching
       something the player has never seen, and it cost the state its ink:
       white measures 3.7:1 on #3085EE and dark ink 4.1:1, so *neither* legible
       option cleared AA, and every selected row on every menu carried it. Same
       hue, three stops down, where white reaches 5.5:1.

       The ramp darkens under the pointer rather than lightening, which is what
       the surface ladder above already does and what keeps the whole ramp
       above 4.5:1 instead of only its rest state. */
    --toy-select: rgb(33, 104, 194);
    --toy-select-hover: rgb(26, 82, 157);
    --toy-select-pressed: rgb(23, 73, 140);
    /* The same blue as *text* on paper, where a filled plate would be the wrong
       shape of emphasis; stepped down until it carries at label size. */
    --toy-select-copy: rgb(23, 83, 156);

    /* Danger: a destructive or failed action. Not the enemy team and not the
       health bar; both of those have their own tokens below. */
    --toy-danger: rgb(199, 46, 42);
    /* Lifted only as far as white still clears 4.5:1 on it. The previous step
       brightened past that and left the hover state of a destructive button as
       the least readable thing on the screen. */
    --toy-danger-hover: rgb(206, 53, 50);
    --toy-danger-pressed: rgb(166, 34, 31);
    --toy-danger-copy: rgb(171, 34, 30);
    --toy-danger-tint: rgb(251, 228, 226);

    /* Team identity. These mirror TeamPalette.cs exactly and must be changed
       with it — the board and the HUD were six different blues and six
       different reds, so a player could not learn which colour was theirs.

       Viewer-relative: the local crew is always the friendly pair on both
       screens. Full chroma on purpose — a muted pair was tried and reverted for
       reading washed out on a bright board, and it also lost CIE76 distance
       under colour-vision deficiency (96 against 123) and dropped the enemy
       card border under 3:1.

       The -copy variants are the pair lifted for the HUD, and they are dark-
       theme ink only: on the match chrome they measure 5.2 and 6.6, but on
       paper they fall to 2.9 and 2.3. Nothing on the front end may use them as
       text. Where the paper theme needs a team colour in type or line work it
       takes the base or -deep step, which clears AA on the whole surface
       ladder. */
    --toy-team-friendly: rgb(47, 67, 245);
    --toy-team-friendly-deep: rgb(11, 34, 235);
    --toy-team-friendly-copy: rgb(110, 123, 255);
    --toy-team-enemy: rgb(248, 53, 71);
    --toy-team-enemy-deep: rgb(237, 8, 29);
    --toy-team-enemy-copy: rgb(255, 107, 122);

    /* Health. A ramp, so the bar reports condition and not only quantity.
       Thresholds and colours are owned by TeamPalette.HealthClassSuffix and
       mirrored here; the world-space bar over the unit reads the same three.
 
       The bar is drawn in fixed segments rather than as one continuous fill —
       that idea is borrowed, because "lost two blocks" is a faster read than
       "lost thirteen percent" — but the colours are this game's, not the
       reference's flat white. */
    --toy-health-high: rgb(92, 200, 122);
    --toy-health-warning: rgb(241, 143, 1);
    --toy-health-critical: rgb(186, 58, 45);

    /* A loss goes cool; a win is simply the brightest white on the screen and
       has no token of its own.
 
       This was a gold once, then the accent. Both were wrong for the same
       reason: a warm, oblique exclamation over a dimmed match is one specific
       game's signature, and reaching for it makes the ending an impression of
       an ending. Orange stays on the rule above the verdict and on the button
       under it, where it is doing its ordinary job. */
    --toy-defeat: rgb(108, 118, 142);

    /* Focus. Deep navy, and deliberately not an alias of --toy-primary: the ring
       and "your next action" must not be the same colour. It belongs to the
       interface rather than to any game state and clears 12:1 on every surface
       in the ladder. Controls on the orange or the blue plate override to
       --toy-text-on-deep, where a navy ring would disappear into the fill. */
    --toy-focus: rgb(24, 32, 38);

    /* Spacing */
    --toy-space-xs: 4px;
    --toy-space-sm: 8px;
    --toy-space-md: 12px;
    --toy-space-lg: 20px;
    --toy-space-xl: 32px;
    --toy-space-control-x: 18px;
    --toy-space-control-top: 12px;
    --toy-space-control-bottom: 12px;
    --toy-space-control-active-top: 12px;
    --toy-space-control-active-bottom: 12px;
    --toy-space-panel: 24px;
    --toy-space-panel-compact: 18px;
    --toy-space-modal: 28px;
    --toy-space-modal-phone: 20px;

    /* Shape and target sizes. Every radius is zero. Overwatch's interface has
       no rounded rectangle anywhere in it: corners are square, and where a
       shape is not square it is a hexagon, a circle, or a parallelogram cut at
       an angle. The three radius tokens are kept so the component library can
       stay declarative and so a future theme can round them again in one edit. */
    --toy-radius-sm: 0;
    --toy-radius-md: 0;
    --toy-radius-lg: 0;

    --toy-target-status: 26px;
    --toy-target-compact: 44px;
    --toy-target: 52px;
    --toy-target-phone: 56px;
    --toy-icon-size: 22px;
    --toy-icon-gap: 12px;
    --toy-scroll-size: 10px;
    /* Width of the angled cap that turns a rectangle into a parallelogram, and
       of the shoulder a slanted plate needs to keep its label off the bevel. */
    --toy-slant: 14px;

    /* Structural depth. Flat: one hairline everywhere, a two-pixel focus ring,
       no offset bottom edge. --toy-accent-rule is the thick coloured bar an
       active or important element wears on one edge, which is how Overwatch
       marks state without filling anything. */
    --toy-stroke-thin: 1px;
    --toy-stroke: 2px;
    --toy-depth-rest: 1px;
    --toy-depth-pressed: 1px;
    --toy-depth-panel: 1px;
    --toy-depth-modal: 1px;
    --toy-focus-width: 1px;
    --toy-focus-emphasis: 2px;
    --toy-accent-rule: 3px;

    /* Type scale. The display family is condensed, so these run larger than the
       same scale would in a normal-width face; at equal point size condensed
       caps read about a step smaller. */
    --toy-type-display: 46px;
    --toy-type-title-lg: 30px;
    --toy-type-title: 21px;
    --toy-type-body-lg: 17px;
    --toy-type-body: 15px;
    --toy-type-label: 13px;
    --toy-type-caption: 12px;
    --toy-type-mono: 22px;

    /* Tracking. Overwatch letterspaces every piece of display type; without it
       condensed caps clot into a single grey mass. Body copy takes almost none. */
    --toy-track-display: 2px;
    --toy-track-title: 1.5px;
    --toy-track-label: 1.6px;
    --toy-track-body: 0.2px;

    /* Interaction timing */
    --toy-motion-fast: 0.09s;
}
